# Track the RECIPE, not the OUTPUT.
#
# Everything here regenerates from the committed scripts against the pinned
# artifacts in data/, so committing 22 MB of solver output would bloat history
# for no reproducibility gain. The generated tables the book renders are
# committed in q2-hdstats-docs (docs/_data/), which is where they are consumed.

results/*
publish/
slurm/logs/

# ...EXCEPT the small text outputs that make_docs_figures.py actually reads.
#
# The rationale above did not survive contact with the code. It says results
# regenerate "from the committed scripts against the pinned artifacts in data/",
# but data/ is ignored three lines below, and data/ itself is unpacked from two
# zips that are ignored at the repo root -- so the chain bottomed out on files
# that exist on one filesystem and nowhere else. It also says the generated
# tables "are committed in docs/_data/, which is where they are consumed"; they
# are consumed from HERE. docs/_data/atacama-lambda-path.tsv is a different
# derivation (lambda / eBIC@gamma=0.3 / edges) from the generator's input
# (lambda1 / sparsity / ebic), so it is not a substitute.
#
# Net effect: nobody with a clean clone could regenerate a single figure. These
# seven files are 103 KB of plain TSV and close that hole. Solver output, .qza
# artifacts and figures stay ignored -- those are the 40 MB the rule is for.
!results/tables/
!results/gglasso/
results/gglasso/*
!results/gglasso/*.tsv

# Input artifacts: unpacked from the two Atacama zips and the gut-to-soil
# tutorial site by scripts/, and listed with checksums in
# q2-hdstats-docs/docs/_data/manifest.tsv. Reproducible, and destined for Zenodo.
data/

# Conda lockfile is committed (it pins the environment); the distribution file
# it derives from is downloaded.
envs/rachis-qiime2-linux-64-conda.yml

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
